Found by the wire lane while VERIFYING the ADR 0020 table for karr #63 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops). Small, probably unreachable, recorded so it is not rediscovered. Adjacent to #102, but a different thing: #102 is pre-existing and encrypt-side, this one is new with ADR 0020 and plaintext-emit-side.

A bare JSON integer literal that overflows a double (1 followed by 400 zeros) is now a float leaf whose NV is +Inf, carrying its 401 digits as its string half. On the ENCRYPT path assert_representable croaks (karr #59 non-finite guard), which is what ADR 0020 documents and what sops does one step earlier at unmarshal time.

The PLAINTEXT emitters (decrypt_file, edit) do not call assert_representable. There the leaf reaches canonical_float_tree, matches $NO_AGREED_FORM, and is returned untouched, so the emitters write it from the dualvars string half. Measured, same tree, baseline f286764^ vs HEAD:

  JSON plaintext emit   quoted string  ->  quoted string   (unchanged)
  YAML plaintext emit   quoted string  ->  BARE number     (changed)

Nothing verifies or fails either way, since a plaintext document has no MAC. The reachability is the reason this is low: the only route is an encrypted document that already carries a bare 400-digit literal in an unencrypted slot. Our own encrypt now refuses to create one, sops cannot unmarshal a JSON document containing one, and a document File::SOPS wrote before 0.003 carries the value QUOTED (it was a str), which the type map still reads as a string. So there may be no document that reaches this at all.

Open question is only whether the bare form is wrong enough to care about, or whether this is a note on ADR 0020s consequences. Do NOT fix by loosening the non-finite guard.

Wire lane. Needs no sops binary to reproduce; the before/after is a two-line emit comparison.
2026-08-21 01:31 DECIDED: consequence of ADR 0020, not a defect. No code change. Recorded in
docs/adr/0020, new section "The overflow row's other half: what the plaintext
emitters write" (63 added lines, nothing removed).

REPRODUCED, both halves, f286764^ vs 3e5e6b0, same tree (Format::JSON->parse of
{"v":1 + 400 zeros), no sops binary needed:

  leaf              POK / detect_type str   ->  NOK+POK / detect_type float, NV +Inf
  Format::JSON->emit  "100...0" quoted      ->  "100...0" quoted   UNCHANGED
  Format::YAML->emit  '100...0' quoted      ->  100...0  bare      CHANGED

Mechanism confirmed directly: canonical_float_tree never calls roundtrips for
this leaf, the $NO_AGREED_FORM branch returns it untouched.

REACHABILITY: the moved row is NOT reachable through decrypt_file or edit.
Both resolve one $format and use it for the parse AND the emit, so a
Format::JSON->parse tree is always written by Format::JSON->emit, which is the
unchanged row. Proven end to end on the one document that gets all the way
through: sops -e --mac-only-encrypted JSON, unencrypted slot hand-edited to the
bare literal (digest does not cover it, so verification PASSES rather than
stopping the read) -> decrypt_file output byte-identical at HEAD and baseline,
the quoted string. Note for the record: without mac_only_encrypted the same
edit fails verification and needs ignore_mac => 1 -- so the MAC is NOT the gate
the body assumed; the format coupling is.

The document itself barely exists, as the body suspected: sops -e refuses such
a JSON plaintext (exit 2), sops -d refuses a hand-edited one (exit 1), HEAD
croaks before writing one, and f286764^ writes it QUOTED (measured:
"v_unencrypted" : "100...0"). Only route to the bare form is a caller emitting
a JSON-parsed tree as YAML itself.

Also measured and worth keeping straight: a YAML *document* is unaffected.
YAML::XS returned the same NOK+POK leaf at f286764^, its plaintext emit was
already bare, and decrypt_file of a sops -e YAML file fails MAC verification
identically before and after. That is karr #102, not this.

Non-finite guard untouched, as the ticket requires. If the quoted form is ever
wanted, the lever is #102's parse-time resolution question for both parsers,
not the emitter (which would have to read a value's text -- ADR 0002).

